---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const yieldCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'rmax*x/(x + k)', label: 'yield response', color: '#ca8a04', width: 2.4 }],
  params: [
    { name: 'rmax', label: 'yield ceiling rₘₐₓ', min: 2, max: 12, step: 0.5, value: 8, unit: 't/ha' },
    { name: 'k', label: 'half-response k', min: 20, max: 200, step: 5, value: 60, unit: 'kg N/ha' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 300, label: 'nitrogen applied (kg/ha)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 12, label: 'yield (t/ha)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/foundational/soil-water-sun">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Farming is managing a photosynthesis factory: <strong>soil</strong> holds water and nutrients,
    <strong>water</strong> carries them, <strong>sun</strong> powers the chemistry, and the crop
    converts all three into yield. The farmer's core insight is that yield obeys
    <strong>diminishing returns</strong> — the first kilo of fertiliser multiplies growth; the
    fiftieth barely helps; beyond some point, more harms the crop and the water table.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="fertiliser response — steep at first, then flat; past the flat part, the extra nitrogen runs off into rivers" widget="plotter" config={yieldCfg} />

  <p>
    Raise the ceiling rₘₐₓ (a better variety, more sun) and the whole curve lifts. Raise k
    (poorer soil holding) and the response slows. Real agronomists fit exactly this curve to
    field trials — the economics of fertiliser is read off the flat part of the graph.
  </p>

  <h2>Other laws the farm obeys</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Liebig's minimum:</strong> growth is limited by the scarcest factor. Piling on nitrogen when phosphorus is short wastes both — the barrel's shortest stave sets the level.</li>
    <li><strong>Water is the swing factor:</strong> a crop can use every nutrient it gets only if water delivers them; roughly 1,000–2,000 litres of water go into a kilo of wheat.</li>
    <li><strong>Soil is alive:</strong> a teaspoon of healthy soil holds more microbes than there are people on Earth. Structure and organic matter are assets that compounding builds slowly.</li>
    <li><strong>The harvest is a withdrawal:</strong> every tonne of grain exports nutrients off the field. Sustainable farming replaces them — the cycle must balance like any other.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This one curve explains a century of agricultural history: synthetic nitrogen (Haber–Bosch,
    1913) pushed the whole curve up and fed billions — and the flat region is where the modern
    environmental debate about runoff and dead zones lives.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FAO fertilizer guidance; Smil, Enriching the Earth; Liebig's law of the minimum (1840).</p>
</TopicPage>
